WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.740 --> 00:00:08.820
Do you remember when Pokemon Go came out?

00:00:08.830 --> 00:00:15.050
We were all walking around with our phones
held up to catch Eevees and Magikarps and

00:00:15.050 --> 00:00:16.940
Pikachus wearing Santa hats.

00:00:16.940 --> 00:00:20.800
It was a good time, if your phone battery
could handle it, because, you know, who doesn't

00:00:20.810 --> 00:00:26.670
love the idea of exploring their world for
invisible, fantastical creatures?

00:00:26.670 --> 00:00:33.430
For centuries, scientists and explorers have
found the same thrill that games like Pokemon

00:00:33.430 --> 00:00:41.989
Go elicit by exploring the microcosmos, a
very real universe of organisms that overlays

00:00:41.989 --> 00:00:45.070
and intersects with our surroundings.

00:00:45.070 --> 00:00:51.899
But it is not augmented reality...it’s actual
reality, visible only under a microscope as

00:00:51.899 --> 00:00:57.980
we examine samples collected from locations
near and far.

00:00:57.980 --> 00:01:03.899
It's an endeavor that has taken to some extreme
environments, like Antarctica, to find just

00:01:03.899 --> 00:01:10.780
what kind of microbe can stake out a life
in almost every conceivable situation.

00:01:10.780 --> 00:01:15.020
But the beauty of the microcosmos is that
it is everywhere.

00:01:15.020 --> 00:01:22.180
You can examine a nearby puddle or hike to
a distant lake, and there the microcosmos will be.

00:01:26.460 --> 00:01:33.980
It's also an incredibly diverse world of organisms
whose contents reflect the location and chemistry

00:01:33.990 --> 00:01:36.750
of the area you select from.

00:01:36.750 --> 00:01:41.920
So the chances are high that any time you
venture out for more samples, something new

00:01:41.920 --> 00:01:47.130
to you will float into view under your microscope
lens.

00:01:47.130 --> 00:01:54.640
Now, before we get to how we collect our samples,
let's start with where we get them from.

00:01:54.640 --> 00:01:59.610
The organisms we showcase on this channel
are all aquatic, inhabitants of water that

00:01:59.610 --> 00:02:06.630
James, our Master of Microscopes, samples
from ponds, lakes, aquariums--just about any

00:02:06.630 --> 00:02:09.989
accessible watery location that seems promising.

00:02:09.989 --> 00:02:16.349
So how do you figure out whether a body of
water might be a good microbe hunting ground?

00:02:16.349 --> 00:02:19.549
One indicator to be on the lookout for is
food.

00:02:19.549 --> 00:02:26.359
Shallow ponds can accumulate leaves and other
plant matter, creating a feast for microbes

00:02:26.359 --> 00:02:30.190
who decompose organic matter for their food.

00:02:30.190 --> 00:02:35.840
On these decomposing leaves, you will often
find a thin layer of sediment.

00:02:35.840 --> 00:02:41.409
And inside that sediment is where you will
find many a microbe.

00:02:41.409 --> 00:02:48.879
Here, James is using a transfer pipette to
take bits and pieces of that sediment, extracting

00:02:48.879 --> 00:02:55.670
it from various points around the decaying
leaves and gathering it into a collection tube.

00:02:55.670 --> 00:03:02.249
Sometimes, he uses a turkey baster instead
because sometimes science requires, you know,

00:03:02.249 --> 00:03:04.799
repurposing other appliances.

00:03:04.799 --> 00:03:11.370
Other compelling signs of life are biofilms,
a mixture of microorganisms that have congregated

00:03:11.370 --> 00:03:17.409
together into a larger, visible mass like
this orange biofilm.

00:03:17.409 --> 00:03:23.370
It might be a little off-putting to look at,
but grossness at a larger scale can obscure

00:03:23.370 --> 00:03:26.559
the beauty of smaller scales.

00:03:26.559 --> 00:03:31.359
Because the area is a bit tough to reach,
James adapted another non-scientific tool

00:03:31.359 --> 00:03:38.739
for his purposes, this time hooking up a collection
tube to a selfie stick so that he can grab

00:03:38.739 --> 00:03:43.419
some of that biofilm for later examination.

00:03:43.419 --> 00:03:48.799
And there are other tools as well, including
this plankton net, which is a cone made of

00:03:48.799 --> 00:03:54.549
mesh that lets water pass through while trapping
organisms inside.

00:03:54.549 --> 00:04:00.749
Our plankton net has a 20 micron mesh size,
so as we pull it out of the water, anything

00:04:00.749 --> 00:04:04.549
larger than 20 microns will be left behind.

00:04:04.549 --> 00:04:10.019
And with every drag through the water, the
samples become more and more concentrated

00:04:10.019 --> 00:04:11.019
with life.

00:04:11.019 --> 00:04:17.259
And by the time we're done, our samples will
have so many organisms that they will deplete

00:04:17.259 --> 00:04:20.930
the dissolved oxygen in the water very quickly.

00:04:20.930 --> 00:04:25.000
So when we collect our microbes in this way,
we have to check them under the microscope

00:04:25.000 --> 00:04:28.160
as soon as possible to keep them alive.

00:04:29.160 --> 00:04:35.159
There are other signs as well that a pond
or lake might be an inspiring microbial source,

00:04:35.159 --> 00:04:39.849
like a greenish color that suggests an excess
of algae.

00:04:39.849 --> 00:04:45.460
But even though the microscopic life we feature
is all aquatic, we don't necessarily have

00:04:45.460 --> 00:04:49.539
to turn to obvious bodies of water to find
them.

00:04:49.539 --> 00:04:55.370
After all, water is not a guaranteed resource
at all times, and a number of microbes have

00:04:55.370 --> 00:04:59.330
evolved their own mechanisms to survive dry
spells.

00:04:59.330 --> 00:05:06.590
When water is scarce, these organisms go dormant,
forming protective cysts that encase their

00:05:06.590 --> 00:05:12.830
resting bodies until it rains or some other
source of hydration awakens them.

00:05:12.830 --> 00:05:18.889
These wet-dry cycles can happen over seasons,
or just over the course of one day as dew

00:05:18.889 --> 00:05:21.729
forms and then evaporates.

00:05:21.729 --> 00:05:27.460
So while we might often focus on ponds and
lakes for finding microbes, it turns out that

00:05:27.460 --> 00:05:33.319
soil, with its periods of wetness, can also
give us some exciting results.

00:05:33.319 --> 00:05:39.000
To find the microorganisms that live there,
we collect the top layer of soil, which is

00:05:39.000 --> 00:05:40.530
around 5 centimeters deep.

00:05:40.530 --> 00:05:45.800
We want to make sure the soil is packed with
plenty of nutrients, so we might make sure

00:05:45.800 --> 00:05:51.020
that there’s a lot of plant litter for the
organisms inside to dine on.

00:05:51.020 --> 00:05:56.289
When air-dried, these samples can be stored
for years without losing a significant number

00:05:56.289 --> 00:06:00.120
of the hardy species that reside in the soil.

00:06:00.120 --> 00:06:05.550
And when it comes time to awaken our sleeping
beauties, we transfer the soil into petri

00:06:05.550 --> 00:06:11.850
dishes and lightly soak them in some distilled
water--not too much though, we don't want

00:06:11.850 --> 00:06:13.539
to flood the soil.

00:06:13.539 --> 00:06:18.379
After 48 hours, we will check on the samples
by tilting the petri dish and collecting some

00:06:18.379 --> 00:06:21.550
of the water to look at under the microscope.

00:06:21.550 --> 00:06:26.560
Of course, these are only a few of the ways
people collect microbes.

00:06:26.560 --> 00:06:33.270
You might find as you start your own journey
that different settings require other means.

00:06:33.270 --> 00:06:38.099
Like this time James wanted to see what diatoms,
ciliates, and flagellates were living in the

00:06:38.099 --> 00:06:43.060
biofilm formed on beach rocks, so he had to
scrape at it.

00:06:43.060 --> 00:06:50.150
It is the microcosmos after all: it just takes
a few steps into nature, some simple tools,

00:06:50.150 --> 00:06:51.189
and a little creativity.

00:06:51.189 --> 00:06:58.889
And the payoff is...well, it’s tiny, but
also infinite.

00:06:58.889 --> 00:07:04.840
Thank you for coming on this journey with
us as we explore the unseen world that surrounds us.

00:07:04.840 --> 00:07:09.999
And thank you of course, and as always, to
all of these amazing people whose names are

00:07:09.999 --> 00:07:12.300
appearing on the screen right now.

00:07:12.300 --> 00:07:16.360
If you like Journey to the Microcosmos, these
are the people who make it possible

00:07:16.360 --> 00:07:18.980
They are our supporters on Patreon.

00:07:18.980 --> 00:07:22.500
And you can find out how to join them in the
description.

00:07:22.509 --> 00:07:27.780
If you want to see more from our Master of
Microscopes, James check out Jam &amp; Germs on

00:07:27.780 --> 00:07:29.050
Instagram.

00:07:29.050 --> 00:07:34.080
And if you want to see more from us, there’s
a bunch of videos just around the corner at

00:07:34.080 --> 00:07:37.600
youtube.com/microcosmos

